#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
hhgpu - HaleHound Apple Silicon system monitor: GPU, per-core CPU, memory, power.

  hhgpu                  live dashboard (ctrl-C to quit)
  hhgpu -s               one-shot snapshot, prints and exits
  hhgpu -i 250           sample interval in ms (default 1000)
  hhgpu -m               hand off to the raw macmon TUI
  hhgpu -V               version
  hhgpu -h               this help

  --size 120x40          force a render size (for screenshots / piping)
  --color MODE           auto (default) | true | 256 | none
  --demo                 synthetic load, to see the look without running a job

REQUIRES: macmon (brew install macmon) and an Apple Silicon Mac.
No Python packages. Runs on the stock macOS python3 (tested on 3.9.6).

WHAT IT SHOWS:
The whole chip on one screen: GPU load, clock and VRAM; every P and E core
with its own bar; memory pressure and swap; all six power rails (cpu, gpu,
ane, ram, display, package); fans, temperatures, net and disk.

One file.  No build step, no pip packages, no venv.  It runs macmon and
draws what macmon reports.

Graphs are braille: 2 samples wide and 4 levels tall per character cell, so a
60x6 graph holds 120 samples at 24 levels of vertical resolution. Colour runs
teal at the floor to hot pink at the ceiling -- height and hue say the same
thing, so a pegged GPU reads as a pink wall at a glance.

COLOUR: 24-bit by default when COLORTERM advertises truecolor, otherwise the
ramp is quantised to the xterm-256 palette. NO_COLOR (no-color.org) or
--color none gives a fully working monochrome dashboard. Colour is NOT
suppressed when stdout is a pipe -- piping into a file is how you screenshot
this thing, and stripping the ramp would defeat the point. Use --color none.

CREDITS / PROVENANCE: all metrics come from `macmon pipe` by vladkens
(MIT, https://github.com/vladkens/macmon), invoked as a subprocess -- hhgpu
contains no macmon code. The braille dot bitmasks and block-element glyphs are
Unicode Standard definitions; the 256-colour cube levels and grey ramp are
xterm's published palette. No code is taken from any other monitor.

